From: accel-virt
Subject: [PATCH] util: a native SetEvent writes the eventfd it is handed

A native build had no SetEvent: the stub logged and answered FALSE, so
ID3D11Fence::SetEventOnCompletion and Flush1's event never told anyone the
GPU got there. accel-virt's executor runs every guest Direct3D 11 session of
a GPU on one DXVK device and holds each batch's ring fence until the GPU has
run it; without an event its worker could only spin on the fence's value.
vkd3d-proton already takes an eventfd where Windows takes an event HANDLE,
and DXVK now does too: setting the event writes 1 to the descriptor, which
is what an eventfd counts and what poll() wakes on.
---
diff --git a/src/util/util_win32_compat.h b/src/util/util_win32_compat.h
index b706dca6..ea430a6e 100644
--- a/src/util/util_win32_compat.h
+++ b/src/util/util_win32_compat.h
@@ -4,6 +4,8 @@
 
 #include <windows.h>
 #include <dlfcn.h>
+#include <stdint.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
 
 #include "log/log.h"
 
@@ -40,9 +42,13 @@ inline BOOL ReleaseSemaphore(
   return FALSE;
 }
 
+// A native build has no event objects: the embedder hands an eventfd where
+// Windows takes an event HANDLE, as vkd3d-proton takes one, and setting the
+// event writes it.
 inline BOOL SetEvent(HANDLE hEvent) {
-  dxvk::Logger::warn("SetEvent not implemented.");
-  return FALSE;
+  const uint64_t one = 1;
+  const int fd = static_cast<int>(reinterpret_cast<intptr_t>(hEvent));
+  return write(fd, &one, sizeof(one)) == static_cast<ssize_t>(sizeof(one));
 }
 
 inline BOOL DuplicateHandle(
